
JOB 3 – Week 1 Joy of computing.week1 debrief

Job 3 focuses on reviewing the concepts learned in Week 1 and understanding how programming can be applied to real-world problems.
It introduces the idea that programming is not separate from engineering; it can be used to solve practical manufacturing and production problems.
1. Algorithm
An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure for solving a problem.
For example, to inspect a manufactured shaft:
Take the diameter measurement.
Compare the measurement with the permitted range.
If it is within the range, accept the shaft.
Otherwise, reject the shaft.
This logical procedure can then be converted into a computer program.
2. Binary Search / Half-Splitting
Binary search is a searching method in which a range is repeatedly divided into two halves.
Suppose there are 100 possible stations and one station is faulty. 
Instead of checking every station one by one, questions can be used to divide the possible range into halves.
For example:
Is the faulty station above 50?
If yes, search between 51–100.
If no, search between 1–50.
Continue dividing the range.
This greatly reduces the number of checks required.
3. Seven-Check Idea
For 100 possible stations, binary search can identify the faulty station in at most 7 yes/no checks, because each check approximately halves the number of possibilities.
This demonstrates an important computer-science idea: 
a good algorithm can solve a problem much more efficiently than checking every possibility individually.
4. Computational Thinking
Computational thinking means solving a problem in a logical and systematic way.
It involves:
Breaking a large problem into smaller steps.
Finding patterns.
Creating logical rules.
Designing an efficient procedure.
Converting the procedure into a program.
5. Application in Production Engineering
Programming and computational thinking can be applied to:
Quality inspection
Machine monitoring
Production planning
Defect detection
Inventory management
Process automation
Therefore, programming is useful not only for computer science but also for production and manufacturing engineering.
Key learning
Job 3 shows that a computer follows the instructions given to it. Therefore, the instructions must be clear, logical and precise. A good algorithm can make an engineering process faster and more efficient
